# AI Assistant Behavior

When helping with this repository:

1. Prefer simple, runnable Python over over-engineered research code. Everything
   (preprocessing, training, online) is Python/PyTorch; MATLAB/Neuracle is reference only.
2. Never hard-code data paths. Load them from `configs/paths.yaml` via
   `src/utils/paths.load_paths()` (env `SHU_2C_ROOT` overrides). If a path is unknown
   or missing, raise a clear error and ask the user to fill `configs/paths.yaml`.
3. Do NOT invent channel order/event ids. Inspect first (`scripts/check_raw_bdf.py`)
   and cross-check labels against the paper `.mat` (truth only, not the data entry).
4. Preserve the tensor convention `[trials, channels, time]`, target `[200, 58, 1000]`.
5. Split by subject, never by trial. No target leakage into source training.
6. Online learning is test-then-update.
7. Comment your code (project requirement): docstrings on every module/script;
   explain EEG-specific / non-obvious choices. Chinese or English is fine.
8. Respect the staged plan (see `docs/PROJECT_STATUS_CURRENT.md`). Current mainline =
   cross-session DG; Steps A/B/1/2 are DONE. Do the requested step only; do NOT jump ahead to
   Step 3 / online / fine-tuning / full CAP-EEGNet / 41-10 / LOSO without an explicit request.
   Mark a step "done" only after its results exist on disk, never on job submission alone.
9. Heavy/GPU work via Slurm ONLY (env `mi_torch_cu118`, fail-fast if CUDA unavailable), never on
   the login node. Smoke-test on a GPU node via `srun` before any full `sbatch`. Write only inside
   the project root; the external dataset under `/share/workspace2/...` is read-only input; sibling
   handoff folders under `/share/home/yuan/SYX/` are read-only references unless the user says so.
10. Expose parameters via YAML configs; keep scripts runnable from the project root;
    save metrics as CSV/JSON with clear `run_id`s.
11. If a requirement is ambiguous, write a small diagnostic script rather than guess.
12. Keep `docs/PROGRESS.md` updated: append a dated entry after each meaningful step
    or decision. It is the project's memory.
